+++ b/domains/findings/pages/no-cluster-service-attachment-under-akasha-is-claimed-by-its-page.finding.ts
@@ -8,5 +8,5 @@ export const noClusterServiceAttachmentUnderAkashaIsClaimedByItsPage = {
   claim:
     "A cluster service names its manifest code by path in a required property, and that naming puts no entry in the index. `file-has-its-page` reads the index alone, so it refuses every attachment. All 49 attachments already under `akasha/` are in the same position, which means each landed with the glass broken and the next lane to carry one in must break it again.",
   evidence:
-    "Measured 2026-09-03 while carrying auth-proxy in. `akasha write` refused `akasha/infrastructure/auth-proxy/auth-proxy.cluster-service.code.attachment.ts` with `no page claims this file` even after `akasha/service-system/cluster-services/pages/auth-proxy.cluster-service.ts` had landed in an earlier commit naming it as `manifestCode`, so the naming was committed and still did not count.\n\nThe check is four lines: `unclaimedAt` in `akasha/checks/code-checks/pages/file-has-its-page/file-has-its-page.code-check.code.ts` returns the refusal wherever `shadow.index.listedByPath(path)` is empty. `listedByPath` in `akasha/pages-system/indexes/index-reading/index-reading.module.code.ts` line 155 reads one file under the index's `path` tree.\n\nThe count is the measurement. 49 files match `*.cluster-service.code.attachment.ts` under `akasha/`, and the index's path tree answers for none of the 49. Spot checks agree: the index folders for `akasha/calendar-sync`, `akasha/infrastructure/voice-inference` and `akasha/service-system/cluster-services/pages/grafana` each hold the page and the manifest and neither the attachment nor the sops file beside it. `deploy/dockerfile-extensions.json` is unindexed on the same footing, under calendar-sync as well as under auth-proxy.\n\n`no-tmp` compounds it. The attachment mounts an `emptyDir` at `/tmp` because the container runs `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true`, and the check refuses the literal. Five attachments already under `akasha/` carry the same line, among them grafana, pgbouncer, pod-janitor, supabase-realtime and tailnet-egress, and the check's own page states `No use of /tmp is kept as permitted`, so there is no exception to ask for. A path inside a pod is not a path on the workstation, which is what the check is about.",
+    "Measured 2026-09-03 while carrying auth-proxy in. `akasha write` refused `akasha/infrastructure/auth-proxy/auth-proxy.cluster-service.code.attachment.ts` with `no page claims this file` even after `akasha/infrastructure/cluster/services/pages/auth-proxy.cluster-service.ts` had landed in an earlier commit naming it as `manifestCode`, so the naming was committed and still did not count.\n\nThe check is four lines: `unclaimedAt` in `akasha/checks/code-checks/pages/file-has-its-page/file-has-its-page.code-check.code.ts` returns the refusal wherever `shadow.index.listedByPath(path)` is empty. `listedByPath` in `akasha/pages-system/indexes/index-reading/index-reading.module.code.ts` line 155 reads one file under the index's `path` tree.\n\nThe count is the measurement. 49 files match `*.cluster-service.code.attachment.ts` under `akasha/`, and the index's path tree answers for none of the 49. Spot checks agree: the index folders for `akasha/calendar-sync`, `akasha/infrastructure/voice-inference` and `akasha/infrastructure/cluster/services/pages/grafana` each hold the page and the manifest and neither the attachment nor the sops file beside it. `deploy/dockerfile-extensions.json` is unindexed on the same footing, under calendar-sync as well as under auth-proxy.\n\n`no-tmp` compounds it. The attachment mounts an `emptyDir` at `/tmp` because the container runs `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true`, and the check refuses the literal. Five attachments already under `akasha/` carry the same line, among them grafana, pgbouncer, pod-janitor, supabase-realtime and tailnet-egress, and the check's own page states `No use of /tmp is kept as permitted`, so there is no exception to ask for. A path inside a pod is not a path on the workstation, which is what the check is about.",
 } as const satisfies Finding
diff --git a/domains/findings/pages/the-akasha-deploy-path-places-no-secret.finding.ts b/domains/findings/pages/the-akasha-deploy-path-places-no-secret.finding.ts
index 18acac7c5eff469b420a86793f36106c235cc4b6..2b5771d320b7c5fed91929be73062759fab7f767 100644
--- a/domains/findings/pages/the-akasha-deploy-path-places-no-secret.finding.ts
+++ b/domains/findings/pages/the-akasha-deploy-path-places-no-secret.finding.ts
@@ -8,5 +8,5 @@ export const theAkashaDeployPathPlacesNoSecret = {
   claim:
     "The deploy path inside akasha applies manifests without ever placing the secrets those manifests demand. `deploy-system/secret/secret.ts` did that work outside akasha and has no counterpart inside it, so a cluster service whose pods read a Secret deploys against whatever the cluster already holds. The old `deploy()` placed secrets between opening the namespace and applying the rest; `putUp` applies straight through.",
   evidence:
-    "Read 2026-09-03 while migrating `deploy-system/`.\n\nWhat the old path did. `deploy-system/secret/secret.ts` exported `demandedBy`, which walked every manifest document for `secretKeyRef`, `secretRef` and `volumes[].secret.secretName` and returned each `{name, key}` demanded; `secretPages`, which listed `*.secret.ts` pages and read `resourceName`/`resourceKey` off each; and `placeSecrets`, which decrypted the sops sidecar beside each page through `sidecarFor`/`valuesIn` and applied one `Opaque` Secret per resource name with `kubectl apply --server-side -f -`. It also refused where two pages claimed one `(resourceName, resourceKey)` pair, and reported `unplaced` demands no page answered.\n\nWhere the old path called it. `deploy-system/deploy/deploy.ts` line 217, inside `deploy()`: the namespace-opening manifests are applied, then `placeSecrets(akasha, plan)`, and only then the rest. The ordering was the point — a pod cannot start against a Secret that is not there yet.\n\nWhat stands inside akasha. `akasha/service-system/cluster-services/workload-deploying/workload-deploying.module.code.ts` `putUp` applies every manifest in `inApplyOrder` and then waits on the rollout; nothing between. `akasha/command-system/commands/deploy/web-putting-up/deploy-web-putting-up.module.code.ts` calls `writeManifests` then `putUp` then `buildInPod`, and names no secret.\n\nWhat I searched. `secretKeyRef`, `secretRef`, `stringData` and `placeSecrets` across `akasha/**/*.ts`: twenty files match, and every one is a `*.cluster-service.code.attachment.ts` or a workflow template that EMITS a manifest referring to a secret. None reads a secret page and applies a Secret. `demandedBy`, `secretPages` and `SecretPage` match nothing in akasha; the only near-name is `DeviceSecretPage` in `akasha/person-system/device-secret-keeping/`, which is a person's device credential and not a cluster Secret.\n\nWhat akasha does carry is the sidecar layer the old file leaned on — `akasha/pages-system/pages/secret/page-secret.module.code.ts` with `page-secret-show`/`-set`/`-clear`/`-reveal` commands. That is the half that reads a value, not the half that puts it into a cluster.\n\nSo `deploy-system/secret/secret.ts` was NOT ablated with its six neighbours. `akasha migration-reach` answers `unreached` for it. It is the only written record of this logic outside the backup, and it stays until the placing is carried in as a module of `service-system`.\n\nNot measured: I did not deploy anything, so I state that no code places a secret, not that a deploy has failed for want of one. A cluster whose Secrets were placed by an earlier run of the old path would deploy fine today.",
+    "Read 2026-09-03 while migrating `deploy-system/`.\n\nWhat the old path did. `deploy-system/secret/secret.ts` exported `demandedBy`, which walked every manifest document for `secretKeyRef`, `secretRef` and `volumes[].secret.secretName` and returned each `{name, key}` demanded; `secretPages`, which listed `*.secret.ts` pages and read `resourceName`/`resourceKey` off each; and `placeSecrets`, which decrypted the sops sidecar beside each page through `sidecarFor`/`valuesIn` and applied one `Opaque` Secret per resource name with `kubectl apply --server-side -f -`. It also refused where two pages claimed one `(resourceName, resourceKey)` pair, and reported `unplaced` demands no page answered.\n\nWhere the old path called it. `deploy-system/deploy/deploy.ts` line 217, inside `deploy()`: the namespace-opening manifests are applied, then `placeSecrets(akasha, plan)`, and only then the rest. The ordering was the point — a pod cannot start against a Secret that is not there yet.\n\nWhat stands inside akasha. `akasha/infrastructure/cluster/services/workload-deploying/workload-deploying.module.code.ts` `putUp` applies every manifest in `inApplyOrder` and then waits on the rollout; nothing between. `akasha/command-system/commands/deploy/web-putting-up/deploy-web-putting-up.module.code.ts` calls `writeManifests` then `putUp` then `buildInPod`, and names no secret.\n\nWhat I searched. `secretKeyRef`, `secretRef`, `stringData` and `placeSecrets` across `akasha/**/*.ts`: twenty files match, and every one is a `*.cluster-service.code.attachment.ts` or a workflow template that EMITS a manifest referring to a secret. None reads a secret page and applies a Secret. `demandedBy`, `secretPages` and `SecretPage` match nothing in akasha; the only near-name is `DeviceSecretPage` in `akasha/person-system/device-secret-keeping/`, which is a person's device credential and not a cluster Secret.\n\nWhat akasha does carry is the sidecar layer the old file leaned on — `akasha/pages-system/pages/secret/page-secret.module.code.ts` with `page-secret-show`/`-set`/`-clear`/`-reveal` commands. That is the half that reads a value, not the half that puts it into a cluster.\n\nSo `deploy-system/secret/secret.ts` was NOT ablated with its six neighbours. `akasha migration-reach` answers `unreached` for it. It is the only written record of this logic outside the backup, and it stays until the placing is carried in as a module of `service-system`.\n\nNot measured: I did not deploy anything, so I state that no code places a secret, not that a deploy has failed for want of one. A cluster whose Secrets were placed by an earlier run of the old path would deploy fine today.",
 } as const satisfies Finding
